```jsx
function Timer() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log("effect", count);
    return () => console.log("cleanup", count);
  }, [count]);

  return <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>{count}</button>;
}
```

questions.explanation:
When `count` updates from 0 to 1, React schedules a re-render. Before executing the effect for the new render, React first executes the cleanup function from the previous effect. Because effects and cleanups capture the values from the render in which they were created (via closure), the previous cleanup captures `count = 0`. Once cleanup finishes, the new effect executes with the updated state `count = 1`. Therefore, the output order is `"cleanup 0"` followed by `"effect 1"`. On final component unmount, the last cleanup will run.

## 📊 How It Works

![Cleanup runs before the next effect](/diagrams/react/effect_deps_cleanup.svg)

