```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ data }) => {
  console.log("Child rendered");
  return <div>{data.name}</div>;
});

function Parent() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const data = { name: "Alice" };

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Click</button>
      <Child data={data} />
    </>
  );
}
```

questions.explanation:
`React.memo` performs a shallow comparison (`Object.is`) of incoming props against previous props to determine if a re-render can be skipped. In this code, the inline object `{ name: "Alice" }` is created as a brand new object in memory during every single render of `Parent`. Even though the internal values (`name: "Alice"`) are identical, the object reference differs (`newObj !== oldObj`), causing `React.memo`'s shallow comparison to return false and force `Child` to re-render. To prevent this re-render, either move the object outside the component or wrap it in `useMemo`.

## 📊 How It Works

![An inline object prop defeats memo](/diagrams/react/memo_reference_trap.svg)

