questions.explanation:
When using array indices as keys (`key={index}`), removing the first item (index 0) causes every subsequent item's key to shift down by one. React identifies elements by their key: it sees that key `0` still exists in the list and reuses the existing DOM node for key `0`. Because uncontrolled inputs manage their own internal DOM state (`value`), the reused DOM input retains its existing typed text. The first item visually displays the value belonging to the previous first item, while the last input disappears from the bottom. Always use stable, unique IDs (e.g., `item.id`) as keys.

## 📊 How It Works

