The Rules of Hooks mandate that hooks must always be called unconditionally at the very top level of React function components or custom hooks. They must never be called inside conditional statements (`if`), loops (`for`, `forEach`), or nested vanilla JavaScript functions. This rule is essential because React does not rely on variable names to identify hooks; instead, it relies on the fixed call order of hooks across renders to maintain an internal singly-linked list of hook fibers associated with the component.

