const [items, setItems] = useState([{ id: 1, done: false }]);

function toggle(id) {
  setItems(items.map(item =>
    item.id === id ? { ...item, done: !item.done } : item
  ));
}
```

questions.explanation:
React relies on reference equality (`Object.is`) to detect state changes and decide whether to schedule a re-render. If you mutate an existing array in place (e.g., with `array.push()` or `array[0].done = true`) and pass that same array reference to `setItems(array)`, React sees that `newArray === oldArray` and may bail out of the update, resulting in no re-render and stale UI. Using `.map()` along with the spread operator (`{ ...item }`) creates a new array reference and new object references for modified items, reliably triggering React's update lifecycle and preserving time-travel debugging/concurrency.

